WEBVTT

00:00:02.360 --> 00:00:06.020
<i>Huh? The court feels smaller, somehow.</i>

00:00:06.430 --> 00:00:10.360
<i>The first time I played here, it felt huge.</i>

00:00:14.180 --> 00:00:17.080
<i>Yeah. I'm calmer than I thought I'd be.</i>

00:00:17.940 --> 00:00:20.370
<i>And my body feels lighter than I expected.</i>

00:00:20.370 --> 00:00:22.870
<i>I feel driven, but not stressed.</i>

00:00:23.470 --> 00:00:26.720
<i>Last year, I ended up losing 
by hitting out of bounds.</i>

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:30.670
<i>But that was the ref's mistake. 
It was actually in.</i>

00:00:31.210 --> 00:00:36.050
<i>My mental state then was good enough 
that I thought I had a chance to win.</i>

00:00:36.280 --> 00:00:39.310
<i>I need to bring that state back 
to reality through my notes,</i>

00:00:40.340 --> 00:00:44.310
<i>so I can start the match 
with that same mentality.</i>

00:00:45.330 --> 00:00:50.070
The Kanto Junior Tennis Tournament 
semifinal round will now begin.

00:00:50.610 --> 00:00:55.070
<i>I'm the challenger, so I just have to 
hit him with everything I've learned!</i>

00:00:55.310 --> 00:00:58.320
Three-set match. Nabae to serve.

00:00:59.830 --> 00:01:01.240
<i>A wide slice!</i>

00:01:01.700 --> 00:01:03.250
<i>His shots really are sharp!</i>

00:01:03.620 --> 00:01:04.490
<i>There it is already!</i>

00:01:04.490 --> 00:01:05.880
<i>But it's my return game.</i>

00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:07.500
<i>1/81!</i>

00:01:10.710 --> 00:01:12.000
Love-15.

00:01:12.570 --> 00:01:14.920
<i>That wasn't like his usual shots.</i>

00:01:15.270 --> 00:01:18.180
<i>Looks like Maruo-kun is in good shape today.</i>

00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:19.680
<i>No, calm down.</i>

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:21.180
<i>Next point!</i>

00:01:27.980 --> 00:01:29.020
Love-30.

00:01:29.020 --> 00:01:30.170
<i>So it's true!</i>

00:01:30.710 --> 00:01:34.490
<i>Since before we started... No, since 
yesterday, something's been different.</i>

00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:38.320
<i>The court feeling small, 
getting weirdly excited...</i>

00:01:39.560 --> 00:01:43.670
<i>My mind is calm, but my body is 
responding. This feeling...</i>

00:01:46.780 --> 00:01:50.100
<i>I'm getting close to the zone!</i>

00:03:22.750 --> 00:03:25.420
<i>He's oddly aggressive, 
and not hesitating at all.</i>

00:03:26.590 --> 00:03:29.760
<i>I have somewhat of a grasp 
of Maruo-kun's characteristics,</i>

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:31.880
<i>so I was going to attack him first, but...</i>

00:03:34.840 --> 00:03:36.210
<i>I can't help it now.</i>

00:03:36.210 --> 00:03:37.440
<i>Things like this happen.</i>

00:03:39.820 --> 00:03:41.840
<i>He's slowing the pace down with a spin?!</i>

00:03:43.450 --> 00:03:44.810
<i>The spin's deep.</i>

00:03:51.270 --> 00:03:52.580
Love-40.

00:03:52.580 --> 00:03:54.880
He pivoted and scored just like that.

00:03:54.880 --> 00:03:57.420
Against Nabae, no less. 
Is he possessed or something?!

00:03:57.790 --> 00:03:59.570
<i>I was right!</i>

00:03:59.570 --> 00:04:02.270
<i>I'm focusing only on what's 
right in front of me,</i>

00:04:02.270 --> 00:04:04.770
<i>and nothing else is going 
through my mind at all.</i>

00:04:09.710 --> 00:04:12.980
<i>At a time like this, I should look 
only at the opponent and the court,</i>

00:04:12.980 --> 00:04:15.600
<i>and leave my shots up to 
my instincts and judgment!</i>

00:04:18.310 --> 00:04:20.040
Game: Maruo. 1-0.

00:04:20.040 --> 00:04:20.930
Yes!

00:04:22.200 --> 00:04:25.320
<i>I was able to break Nabae-kun's 
serve right off the bat!</i>

00:04:25.320 --> 00:04:29.410
What?! He's off to an unbelievable start.

00:04:29.410 --> 00:04:31.680
Maruo's definitely winning the battle,

00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:34.290
but no lucky streak lasts this long.

00:04:34.290 --> 00:04:38.820
I figured he'd start out in good condition, 
but I didn't think it'd be <i>this</i> good.

00:04:43.020 --> 00:04:46.300
<i>He's close to the optimal 
condition he was in against Ide.</i>

00:04:49.320 --> 00:04:53.310
Aggressive is Ideal

00:04:55.810 --> 00:04:58.810
<i>I'll let my instincts direct my serves, too.</i>

00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:01.400
<i>His flat serve went in!</i>

00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:03.830
<i>Moving up immediately, huh?</i>

00:05:07.970 --> 00:05:09.300
Love-15.

00:05:12.500 --> 00:05:15.290
<i>Even if it's luck, winning a point is big.</i>

00:05:15.290 --> 00:05:17.170
<i>That was just bad luck.</i>

00:05:17.170 --> 00:05:18.340
<i>I won't read into it.</i>

00:05:22.810 --> 00:05:25.520
<i>Maruo-kun is in his best possible condition.</i>

00:05:26.180 --> 00:05:27.970
<i>It won't be easy to break.</i>

00:05:30.550 --> 00:05:32.120
15-all.

00:05:32.120 --> 00:05:35.680
<i>But it's not as though I can't even score a point.</i>

00:05:37.960 --> 00:05:40.080
Out. 15-30.

00:05:40.700 --> 00:05:44.490
<i>He may have luck, but his aggressiveness 
will lead to mistakes, too.</i>

00:05:46.460 --> 00:05:51.340
<i>And even players like Maruo-kun 
who have no set attack pattern</i>

00:05:51.340 --> 00:05:53.910
<i>have tendencies on a given day
that come to light over time.</i>

00:05:57.860 --> 00:06:01.920
<i>He's returning most of my deep 
spin shots in the same way.</i>

00:06:04.420 --> 00:06:08.170
<i>And so far, he mostly hits down
the line when he goes for the point.</i>

00:06:10.250 --> 00:06:14.910
<i>The first volley relies heavily on the basics, 
so it goes deep toward open areas.</i>

00:06:18.570 --> 00:06:20.650
Game: Maruo. 2-0.

00:06:20.650 --> 00:06:21.970
<i>Yes, I held!</i>

00:06:21.970 --> 00:06:25.730
<i>When a player displays 
abilities beyond his strength,</i>

00:06:26.190 --> 00:06:28.290
<i>his true nature shows.</i>

00:06:29.050 --> 00:06:32.450
<i>Right now, I can make this valuable data mine.</i>

00:06:39.710 --> 00:06:41.380
<i>Use 1/100 here!</i>

00:06:44.480 --> 00:06:45.850
Love-15.

00:06:45.850 --> 00:06:48.380
<i>Nevertheless, he's impressive.</i>

00:06:48.380 --> 00:06:50.880
Nabae Yu
Maruo Eiichiro

00:06:52.940 --> 00:06:57.270
<i>I'll trust my instincts, and hit a strong 
flat shot with my 1/81 control!</i>

00:06:58.440 --> 00:07:01.270
<i>I can't afford to let him 
widen the gap any further.</i>

00:07:04.760 --> 00:07:05.980
All right!

00:07:05.980 --> 00:07:07.920
Game: Maruo. 3-0.

00:07:09.930 --> 00:07:12.780
Whoa! Maruo broke serve again?!

00:07:12.780 --> 00:07:15.330
Don't tell me... Is he going to...

00:07:15.330 --> 00:07:18.750
No, no. There's no way Nabae 
would go down like this.

00:07:21.980 --> 00:07:26.120
<i>My body's automatically going for the best 
shots, even when I'm not thinking about it.</i>

00:07:26.120 --> 00:07:30.010
<i>There's no mistake. I'm playing 
the best I ever have.</i>

00:07:30.850 --> 00:07:35.760
<i>It's proof that all my training to change 
the pace is ingrained in me now.</i>

00:07:38.400 --> 00:07:39.140
Time.

00:07:42.850 --> 00:07:45.860
Nabae Yu
Maruo Eiichiro

00:07:43.450 --> 00:07:45.350
Game: Maruo. 4-0.

00:07:47.110 --> 00:07:50.740
Nabae Yu
Maruo Eiichiro

00:07:47.510 --> 00:07:49.410
Game: Nabae. 4-1.

00:07:49.410 --> 00:07:50.740
Maruo leads.

00:07:50.950 --> 00:07:52.790
<i>No matter how well I'm playing,</i>

00:07:52.790 --> 00:07:55.770
<i>it's impossible to break 
Nabae-kun's serve every time.</i>

00:07:56.520 --> 00:07:58.200
<i>So this is fine.</i>

00:07:58.420 --> 00:07:59.600
<i>4-1...</i>

00:07:59.600 --> 00:08:01.680
<i>And I'm in the lead after 
breaking serve twice.</i>

00:08:02.460 --> 00:08:05.770
<i>If I can win this set, I can 
afford to take more risks.</i>

00:08:05.770 --> 00:08:09.290
<i>For now, just going in boldly for 
the score is proving effective.</i>

00:08:09.290 --> 00:08:10.460
Time.

00:08:11.160 --> 00:08:16.160
<i>The higher-risk shots that I couldn't 
pull off last year are working now.</i>

00:08:16.570 --> 00:08:18.150
<i>That's why I'm winning points.</i>

00:08:18.820 --> 00:08:22.850
<i>This excellent condition is beyond what 
I expected, but it's no coincidence.</i>

00:08:24.970 --> 00:08:27.270
<i>It's the result of training 
for this very moment.</i>

00:08:30.400 --> 00:08:31.540
<i>No...</i>

00:08:31.540 --> 00:08:34.460
<i>It's not like first serves 
make it in 100% of the time.</i>

00:08:34.460 --> 00:08:36.880
<i>There are bound to be mistakes 
when playing offensively.</i>

00:08:41.010 --> 00:08:42.390
Love-15.

00:08:42.390 --> 00:08:43.370
What?!

00:08:44.070 --> 00:08:47.400
Whoa. That's the most Nabae-like 
attack we've seen in a while.

00:08:47.400 --> 00:08:51.090
That's true. He was getting 
crushed in his return game.

00:08:53.550 --> 00:08:57.780
<i>Huh? Something is definitely
different from before.</i>

00:08:57.780 --> 00:08:58.870
<i>Why?</i>

00:08:59.530 --> 00:09:00.590
<i>Don't tell me...</i>

00:09:00.590 --> 00:09:05.060
<i>Was it because I thought about 
the future for a second?</i>

00:09:05.740 --> 00:09:08.650
<i>Being in the zone means 
focusing only on the present.</i>

00:09:08.650 --> 00:09:11.860
<i>It's obvious that it's not good to 
think about the past and future.</i>

00:09:12.420 --> 00:09:15.400
<i>I have to think only of right now.</i>

00:09:17.340 --> 00:09:18.010
Fault.

00:09:20.620 --> 00:09:22.300
<i>This isn't bad.</i>

00:09:22.300 --> 00:09:24.150
<i>I just have to clear my mind more.</i>

00:09:26.570 --> 00:09:28.380
<i>Focus only on this shot right now.</i>

00:09:30.200 --> 00:09:32.180
Fault. Double fault.

00:09:32.180 --> 00:09:33.340
Love-30.

00:09:34.230 --> 00:09:37.630
<i>Th-That wasn't clearing my mind. 
I wasn't thinking at all!</i>

00:09:37.960 --> 00:09:41.020
A flat on the second serve is kind of reckless.

00:09:41.020 --> 00:09:42.430
Is Maruo okay?

00:09:46.200 --> 00:09:47.560
<i>He's moved up a step!</i>

00:09:47.560 --> 00:09:49.270
<i>He saw my spin serve coming!</i>

00:09:52.310 --> 00:09:53.530
Love-40.

00:09:55.300 --> 00:09:57.990
<i>Looks like his gears are 
falling out of alignment.</i>

00:09:58.790 --> 00:10:01.450
<i>How could I not adapt to those movements?</i>

00:10:01.450 --> 00:10:04.620
<i>It's proof that my field of 
vision narrows during my serve.</i>

00:10:04.870 --> 00:10:06.330
<i>Don't panic.</i>

00:10:06.330 --> 00:10:09.370
<i>I should still be near the ideal condition.</i>

00:10:09.370 --> 00:10:11.890
<i>It doesn't have to be ideal all the time.</i>

00:10:11.890 --> 00:10:14.000
<i>But at the crucial moments, at least...</i>

00:10:14.420 --> 00:10:15.500
<i>Concentrate!</i>

00:10:17.370 --> 00:10:20.970
<i>Everyone relies on past successes 
when they start feeling anxious.</i>

00:10:23.010 --> 00:10:25.350
<i>Now is when that data from 
earlier can work for me.</i>

00:10:26.200 --> 00:10:26.980
<i>Down the line!</i>

00:10:28.520 --> 00:10:29.530
<i>He read me!</i>

00:10:32.730 --> 00:10:34.630
Game: Nabae. 4-2.

00:10:34.630 --> 00:10:35.540
Maruo leads.

00:10:35.540 --> 00:10:38.820
There it is! Nabae already broke back!

00:10:38.820 --> 00:10:40.500
Bro...

00:10:39.880 --> 00:10:41.840
It's getting interesting now.

00:10:41.840 --> 00:10:43.830
Nabae, we've been waiting for this!

00:10:55.090 --> 00:10:56.490
<i>Damn.</i>

00:10:56.490 --> 00:10:58.010
<i>I get it.</i>

00:10:58.010 --> 00:11:00.780
<i>Even if I was able to clear my mind at first,</i>

00:11:00.780 --> 00:11:04.930
<i>it's difficult not to care 
about the score when it's 4-1.</i>

00:11:05.290 --> 00:11:08.130
<i>That being said, if I focus too 
much on clearing my mind,</i>

00:11:08.130 --> 00:11:11.770
<i>important things also slip from 
my mind, and I lose balance.</i>

00:11:12.210 --> 00:11:16.660
<i>In other words, it's not easy to keep 
yourself in the ideal condition</i>

00:11:16.660 --> 00:11:18.820
<i>at all times during a match.</i>

00:11:19.280 --> 00:11:23.280
Nabae Yu
Maruo Eiichiro

00:11:19.850 --> 00:11:22.360
<i>I was able to get this far thanks to luck.</i>

00:11:23.970 --> 00:11:26.820
<i>I can't rely on being in the zone anymore.</i>

00:11:27.240 --> 00:11:31.300
<i>It's better to shift all the way 
into a realistic style of play.</i>

00:11:32.100 --> 00:11:37.000
Center      Front          Wide

00:11:32.290 --> 00:11:34.250
<i>Consider the data I've collected so far,</i>

00:11:34.250 --> 00:11:36.720
<i>and predict the most likely direction and shot.</i>

00:11:37.450 --> 00:11:40.420
<i>I'll give over 60% of my 
attention to the wide serve.</i>

00:11:44.490 --> 00:11:45.800
<i>Center?!</i>

00:11:45.800 --> 00:11:47.090
15-love.

00:11:48.790 --> 00:11:51.110
<i>He really is incredible.</i>

00:11:51.110 --> 00:11:54.190
<i>I'm impressed I broke his serve twice.</i>

00:11:54.580 --> 00:11:56.600
<i>Wait, this isn't the time for that!</i>

00:11:56.600 --> 00:11:59.290
<i>I have to make sure he 
doesn't surprise me again!</i>

00:12:00.050 --> 00:12:01.320
<i>Watch carefully.</i>

00:12:05.070 --> 00:12:05.890
Fault.

00:12:06.980 --> 00:12:08.470
<i>That was close.</i>

00:12:08.470 --> 00:12:12.580
<i>But even Nabae-kun will go to
a second serve 30% of the time.</i>

00:12:13.110 --> 00:12:16.170
<i>I'll make that my opportunity 
by changing the pace.</i>

00:12:18.290 --> 00:12:19.130
<i>All right...</i>

00:12:19.750 --> 00:12:22.880
<i>A slow spin and 1/64 control!</i>

00:12:24.760 --> 00:12:28.010
<i>Now slice toward that same direction.</i>

00:12:29.890 --> 00:12:33.770
<i>It's easy to disrupt the opponent's 
timing by hitting different shots</i>

00:12:33.770 --> 00:12:35.770
<i>to the same area at the same speed.</i>

00:12:37.270 --> 00:12:40.490
<i>I'll return this slightly shallow 
shot in the same direction,</i>

00:12:40.490 --> 00:12:42.930
<i>but this time with a fast flat shot!</i>

00:12:45.870 --> 00:12:46.910
15-all.

00:12:46.910 --> 00:12:48.020
<i>Yes!</i>

00:12:48.020 --> 00:12:50.560
<i>I was able to score by changing the pace!</i>

00:12:51.040 --> 00:12:55.010
<i>Even if I'm not in the ideal condition 
during my return game, I can still score!</i>

00:12:55.990 --> 00:12:59.250
<i>Three different shots 
along the same direction...</i>

00:12:59.710 --> 00:13:02.420
<i>This style feels more 
like the usual Maruo-kun.</i>

00:13:05.120 --> 00:13:06.360
<i>First serve's in!</i>

00:13:07.550 --> 00:13:10.560
<i>First, I'll put a weak spin on a deep 
forehand shot down the line!</i>

00:13:12.490 --> 00:13:15.810
<i>Next, I'll pivot immediately 
and hit a fast flat shot!</i>

00:13:19.340 --> 00:13:20.710
30-15.

00:13:21.850 --> 00:13:23.820
Here comes Nabae's counterattack!

00:13:23.820 --> 00:13:27.630
Huh?! I thought Maruo's shot was good, 
but it was hit back even harder!

00:13:27.630 --> 00:13:29.870
<i>Wh-What was that?</i>

00:13:30.640 --> 00:13:33.830
<i>It's not like my shot was good 
enough to turn the match around,</i>

00:13:34.060 --> 00:13:38.290
<i>but it wasn't weak enough to 
be shot down in one strike.</i>

00:13:42.980 --> 00:13:44.550
40-15.

00:13:54.100 --> 00:13:55.180
<i>Again!</i>

00:13:55.750 --> 00:13:57.550
Game: Nabae. 4-3.

00:13:57.550 --> 00:13:58.610
Maruo leads.

00:13:59.030 --> 00:14:00.310
Way to hold!

00:14:00.310 --> 00:14:01.990
Whoa, this is intense!

00:14:01.990 --> 00:14:04.990
Here we go! That's how you 
play the best tennis in Japan!

00:14:10.110 --> 00:14:12.090
<i>After that, I sort of understand now.</i>

00:14:12.950 --> 00:14:16.750
<i>Nabae-kun is targeting my flat shots.</i>

00:14:17.130 --> 00:14:19.730
<i>A flat is a strong shot, 
so if you return it well,</i>

00:14:19.730 --> 00:14:21.960
<i>it'll come back as an even stronger shot.</i>

00:14:21.960 --> 00:14:24.840
<i>And my method of changing the pace</i>

00:14:21.960 --> 00:14:28.990
Flat     Since this is a straight shot, it has strength and speed. 
It's very aggressive, but it's prone to miss.

00:14:21.960 --> 00:14:28.990
Slice     This shot is slow and doesn't bounce away. 
It's the most stable shot, but difficult to drop the speed.

00:14:24.840 --> 00:14:28.640
<i>revolves around how I utilize my flat shot.</i>

00:14:29.130 --> 00:14:32.240
<i>He's going straight for my crucial move.</i>

00:14:32.810 --> 00:14:34.390
<i>Even though it's more risky,</i>

00:14:34.390 --> 00:14:37.090
<i>the reason he's targeting it is most likely</i>

00:14:37.900 --> 00:14:41.230
<i>to destroy my method of 
changing the pace at its root.</i>

00:14:41.890 --> 00:14:46.080
<i>He targeted the heart of my tennis, 
fully knowing what my style was.</i>

00:14:46.600 --> 00:14:48.560
<i>This is how Nabae-kun plays tennis.</i>

00:14:54.740 --> 00:14:58.750
Nabae Yu
Maruo Eiichiro

00:14:54.990 --> 00:14:56.490
<i>Don't get timid.</i>

00:14:56.490 --> 00:14:58.750
<i>I'm still one break ahead.</i>

00:14:59.430 --> 00:15:04.040
<i>There are two ways to prevent Nabae-kun 
from hitting that powerful flat shot return.</i>

00:15:04.040 --> 00:15:12.800
1.) Don't hit flat shots.
2.) If I do, be sure it'll win the point.

00:15:04.770 --> 00:15:07.670
<i>The first is to not hit flat shots.</i>

00:15:08.260 --> 00:15:12.170
<i>The second is, if I do hit one, 
to be sure it'll win the point.</i>

00:15:13.260 --> 00:15:15.480
<i>But if I don't go for any flat shots,</i>

00:15:15.480 --> 00:15:17.830
<i>I won't be able to avoid playing passively.</i>

00:15:18.340 --> 00:15:19.680
<i>Considering that,</i>

00:15:19.680 --> 00:15:23.810
<i>it may be better to continue using my 
flat shot, the crux of my change-of-pace.</i>

00:15:24.370 --> 00:15:26.750
<i>Basically, I just have to go for</i>

00:15:26.750 --> 00:15:29.260
<i>a winner with my flat shot 
when I have the chance!</i>

00:15:30.130 --> 00:15:30.970
Time.

00:15:32.330 --> 00:15:35.220
<i>First of all, I have to get the 
upper hand with my first serve.</i>

00:15:35.830 --> 00:15:41.080
<i>Then, I'll use control more precise 
than 1/81 and go for the flat shot.</i>

00:15:41.710 --> 00:15:45.840
<i>This situation is similar to the end of 
the match I lost to Nabae-kun last year.</i>

00:15:46.600 --> 00:15:49.340
<i>But I'm different from who I was back then.</i>

00:15:51.730 --> 00:15:53.420
<i>I have to hold serve here!</i>

00:15:53.840 --> 00:15:56.040
<i>Before I aim for a winner with my stroke,</i>

00:15:56.040 --> 00:15:58.100
<i>it's best if I can win on my serve!</i>

00:15:59.150 --> 00:15:59.980
<i>Go!</i>

00:16:05.960 --> 00:16:08.180
Out. 15-love.

00:16:08.180 --> 00:16:11.860
<i>That was a good serve on a good 
course, and yet it was still close.</i>

00:16:12.310 --> 00:16:14.480
<i>He's gotten much faster since last year.</i>

00:16:14.980 --> 00:16:17.570
<i>And he's probably not even 
playing with all he's got.</i>

00:16:18.240 --> 00:16:21.540
<i>His serves are probably what improved most.</i>

00:16:22.420 --> 00:16:27.990
Flat shots are the crux of Bro's change-of-pace...
and he's targeting them, isn't he?

00:16:27.990 --> 00:16:29.800
Yeah, that's right.

00:16:33.480 --> 00:16:36.260
If Nabae's targeting that...

00:16:36.810 --> 00:16:41.180
That isn't only the most efficient strategy.

00:16:41.180 --> 00:16:45.560
It's also an opportunity to show that, 
based on ability alone, Nabae is better.

00:16:47.140 --> 00:16:48.820
15-all.

00:16:49.300 --> 00:16:52.770
<i>That was weak. I can't hit 
a winner with a shot like that.</i>

00:16:52.770 --> 00:16:56.920
<i>Even if I can't hit a winner, I have 
to do something to break his stance.</i>

00:16:57.610 --> 00:17:00.680
<i>But before that, I have to 
score on my first serve.</i>

00:17:02.980 --> 00:17:06.240
<i>If it gets returned, react swiftly with 
something other than a flat shot!</i>

00:17:08.550 --> 00:17:11.260
<i>And when I get my chance,</i>

00:17:11.260 --> 00:17:12.760
<i>go for the score with a flat!</i>

00:17:16.970 --> 00:17:18.070
<i>It came back!</i>

00:17:18.070 --> 00:17:21.220
<i>But now that I have another chance...</i>

00:17:21.220 --> 00:17:22.090
<i>Again!</i>

00:17:27.860 --> 00:17:29.960
Out. 30-15.

00:17:31.220 --> 00:17:33.210
<i>That was close again.</i>

00:17:33.210 --> 00:17:37.740
<i>He was able to control that shot even 
though he wasn't positioned for it.</i>

00:17:38.170 --> 00:17:41.750
<i>It's a very Nabae-kun-like shot, 
as if the racket were part of his body.</i>

00:17:42.420 --> 00:17:47.020
<i>With his flexible wrists and racket handling, 
he can pretty much hit any shot</i>

00:17:47.020 --> 00:17:49.930
<i>as long as he can make contact with the ball.</i>

00:17:51.100 --> 00:17:53.640
<i>He's striking with flat shots 
instead of shying away.</i>

00:17:54.130 --> 00:17:56.940
<i>His choice of strategy is more 
optimistic than it used to be.</i>

00:17:57.970 --> 00:18:00.760
<i>Maybe something changed his mentality.</i>

00:18:03.620 --> 00:18:05.670
Game: Maruo. 5-3.

00:18:05.670 --> 00:18:06.650
Maruo leads.

00:18:07.270 --> 00:18:11.150
Nabae Yu
Maruo Eiichiro

00:18:08.350 --> 00:18:11.150
There was a lot of advancing and 
retreating in that eighth game.

00:18:11.610 --> 00:18:16.790
Bro was somehow able to hold serve, 
since he was able to get his first serves in.

00:18:16.790 --> 00:18:21.480
Yeah, it's true that he's gone 
all-out during the first set,

00:18:21.480 --> 00:18:24.940
but at the same time, he's also been 
reminded of Nabae's abilities.

00:18:25.870 --> 00:18:28.910
<i>When I think I have the 
advantage, I actually don't.</i>

00:18:28.910 --> 00:18:31.470
<i>And when I think I scored, it gets hit back.</i>

00:18:32.290 --> 00:18:36.760
<i>It's good that my first serves are going in, 
but if my success rate drops at all,</i>

00:18:36.760 --> 00:18:38.880
<i>he can break my serve at any time.</i>

00:18:39.250 --> 00:18:40.790
<i>Considering that,</i>

00:18:40.790 --> 00:18:44.890
<i>I want to somehow break his serve 
on my return game and take this set!</i>

00:18:45.410 --> 00:18:47.650
<i>It's normal to be scored 
on during a return game.</i>

00:18:48.080 --> 00:18:50.260
<i>I have to utilize my lead to fight back!</i>

00:18:51.590 --> 00:18:52.520
<i>Center!</i>

00:18:55.770 --> 00:18:57.010
15-love.

00:18:58.820 --> 00:19:00.790
<i>Damn. It was wide.</i>

00:19:00.790 --> 00:19:03.170
<i>The success rate of his first serves is rising.</i>

00:19:03.720 --> 00:19:06.080
<i>Then where will he aim next?</i>

00:19:07.580 --> 00:19:09.400
<i>Wide. I was right!</i>

00:19:10.290 --> 00:19:11.830
<i>The moment I have the 
upper hand, strike!</i>

00:19:15.930 --> 00:19:17.440
30-love.

00:19:17.440 --> 00:19:18.790
<i>No way.</i>

00:19:18.790 --> 00:19:23.310
<i>I was able to read his moves perfectly, 
and I managed to break his stance,</i>

00:19:23.310 --> 00:19:24.850
<i>but he still got me!</i>

00:19:28.930 --> 00:19:30.100
40-love.

00:19:35.800 --> 00:19:37.740
Game: Nabae. 5-4.

00:19:37.740 --> 00:19:38.800
Maruo leads.

00:19:40.590 --> 00:19:43.060
<i>He held serve, and I couldn't even win one point.</i>

00:19:45.030 --> 00:19:46.310
<i>In my return game,</i>

00:19:46.310 --> 00:19:48.960
<i>he can score on me before I have 
a chance to hit a flat shot.</i>

00:19:49.540 --> 00:19:52.620
<i>I didn't feel like I could 
break his serve at all.</i>

00:19:53.270 --> 00:19:57.410
<i>In that case, I have to hold 
serve and win the first set.</i>

00:19:57.840 --> 00:20:01.880
Nabae Yu
Maruo Eiichiro

00:19:58.130 --> 00:20:01.880
<i>But I'm sure Nabae-kun will 
give it his all here, too.</i>

00:20:02.220 --> 00:20:05.970
<i>And it's not going to be as 
easy to hold as it has been.</i>

00:20:06.700 --> 00:20:09.270
<i>Then... what should I do?</i>

00:20:10.360 --> 00:20:11.300
While you're at it,

00:20:11.300 --> 00:20:15.400
you might as well do something bold 
that you'd never do under pressure.

00:20:15.400 --> 00:20:17.780
L-Like what?

00:20:17.780 --> 00:20:23.400
Like aiming for the lines instead of 
dividing up the court into squares.

00:20:23.400 --> 00:20:27.530
The lines? Wouldn't that 
be an unnecessary risk?

00:20:27.530 --> 00:20:30.120
You can't know until you try!

00:20:30.120 --> 00:20:32.730
And aim for aces on all your serves!

00:20:32.730 --> 00:20:34.540
Are you serious?

00:20:34.780 --> 00:20:37.310
<i>We talked about a lot of options,</i>

00:20:37.310 --> 00:20:39.230
<i>but if he's catching up like this...</i>

00:20:39.630 --> 00:20:42.440
<i>This is the time to try it, 
while I'm still in the lead.</i>

00:20:42.440 --> 00:20:44.800
<i>It's worth trying!</i>

00:20:45.290 --> 00:20:46.280
Time.

00:20:46.280 --> 00:20:49.060
<i>My opponent is Japan's best player. 
I'll try whatever it takes.</i>

00:20:57.780 --> 00:21:02.030
If you're going to be bold, 
it means nothing if you're careless.

00:21:02.030 --> 00:21:06.060
You've got to use your own experiences, 
and what makes you you,

00:21:06.060 --> 00:21:08.500
to make the game go in your favor.

00:21:09.430 --> 00:21:10.500
<i>All right...</i>

00:21:10.990 --> 00:21:17.750
2) Go in for the score
1) If possible, take a shot


00:21:10.990 --> 00:21:17.750
3) Go in for the score 
2) If possible, take a shot 
1) Observe

00:21:11.400 --> 00:21:12.940
<i>I'll use the two-phase method</i>

00:21:12.940 --> 00:21:16.680
<i>I learned in Florida to go for the win
from the baseline when possible.</i>

00:21:18.090 --> 00:21:22.620
<i>I'll win points by increasing 
the risk of those shots</i>

00:21:22.620 --> 00:21:25.670
<i>to the utmost limit by changing the pace.</i>

00:21:27.030 --> 00:21:29.350
<i>I'll increase my ball speed overall,</i>

00:21:29.350 --> 00:21:34.610
<i>but when I hit a slower ball, I'll control 
it with pinpoint accuracy as best I can.</i>

00:21:35.240 --> 00:21:39.190
<i>This is close to the ultimate 
tennis that I strive for.</i>

00:21:40.100 --> 00:21:43.610
<i>Since I'm not scoring aces 
during my service games now,</i>

00:21:43.610 --> 00:21:46.260
<i>I have to change something or 
I won't be able to hold serve.</i>

00:21:47.910 --> 00:21:51.590
<i>I expected to make more mistakes in this game.</i>

00:21:51.590 --> 00:21:53.660
<i>But for me to take this set,</i>

00:21:54.060 --> 00:21:55.410
<i>I have to attack boldly!</i>

00:21:57.670 --> 00:22:00.350
<i>I got it to the corner, 
but it came right back to me!</i>

00:22:00.350 --> 00:22:03.310
<i>Now I'll just barely keep it straight and...</i>

00:22:03.310 --> 00:22:04.340
<i>add spin!</i>

00:22:12.610 --> 00:22:13.930
Love-15.

00:22:15.110 --> 00:22:16.980
<i>It's not just my flat shots.</i>

00:22:17.450 --> 00:22:19.910
<i>He's trying to score on my spin shots, too?!</i>

00:24:00.000 --> 00:24:25.020
Next Time

00:24:00.520 --> 00:24:04.040
After all this, he's going for a 
preemptive attack I have no data on.

00:24:04.040 --> 00:24:07.000
But this isn't how Maruo-kun truly plays.

00:24:07.000 --> 00:24:09.770
It's more reckless than it is unpredictable.

00:24:09.770 --> 00:24:13.380
He continues to reign as Japan's best player.

00:24:14.020 --> 00:24:17.040
That's exactly why I want to beat him.

00:24:17.580 --> 00:24:20.570
So for now, I'm going to do everything I can!

00:24:21.110 --> 00:24:22.960
Next time on <i>Baby Steps</i>,

00:24:21.520 --> 00:24:25.020
Recklessness and Surprise

00:24:22.960 --> 00:24:24.650
"Recklessness and Surprise."

00:24:25.210 --> 00:24:31.000
End
